          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "3012bc61-595d-5e30-83c1-25dd515d5971"
          ]
        }
      ],
      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & k \\\\ -2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|)+((1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}|)+((k)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((-1)\\cdot ((0)\\cdot (0)-(3)\\cdot (-1)))+((1)\\cdot ((-2)\\cdot (0)-(3)\\cdot (3)))+((k)\\cdot ((-2)\\cdot (-1)-(0)\\cdot (3)))\\\\ &=((-1)\\cdot (3))+((1)\\cdot (-9))+((k)\\cdot (2))\\\\ &=(-3)+(-9)+(2k)\\\\ &=2k-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k-12&=0\\\\ 2k&=12\\\\ k&=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=6.$",
