      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ k & 0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((-2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}|)+((3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}k & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}|)+((0)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((-2)\\cdot ((0)\\cdot (2)-(2)\\cdot (2)))+((3)\\cdot ((k)\\cdot (2)-(2)\\cdot (-3)))+((0)\\cdot ((k)\\cdot (2)-(0)\\cdot (-3)))\\\\ &=((-2)\\cdot (-4))+((3)\\cdot (2k+6))+((0)\\cdot (2k))\\\\ &=(8)+(6k+18)+(0)\\\\ &=6k+26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6k+26&=0\\\\ 6k&=-26\\\\ k&=-\\frac{13}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\frac{13}{3}.$",
