  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186105908,
      "id": "26c84e5b-97a7-5116-a90c-e4f011e87cf0",
      "title": "Determining Whether Three Vectors are Coplanar"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "7131b9b7-6dc7-594b-b5e9-6ddf07d407d9",
      ":question/problem": "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=\u27e80,-3,-2\u27e9$, $b=\u27e81,k,0\u27e9$, and $c=\u27e8-3,-3,2\u27e9$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "d39d123b-136c-5074-a1c8-8bf13367aa78",
          ":answer-field/key": "field-1",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/id": "780ced5d-cf0d-5734-9668-76bc95a2e447",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "2"
            }
          ],
          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "780ced5d-cf0d-5734-9668-76bc95a2e447"
          ]
        }
      ],
      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & k & 0 \\\\ -3 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}|)+((3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}|)+((-2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & k \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot ((k)\\cdot (2)-(0)\\cdot (-3)))+((3)\\cdot ((1)\\cdot (2)-(0)\\cdot (-3)))+((-2)\\cdot ((1)\\cdot (-3)-(k)\\cdot (-3)))\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot (2k))+((3)\\cdot (2))+((-2)\\cdot (3k-3))\\\\ &=(0)+(6)+(6-6k)\\\\ &=12-6k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-6k&=0\\\\ -6k&=-12\\\\ k&=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=2.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy"
    },
