    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "b28a16f4-db8d-58ee-800a-618a472f974b",
      ":question/problem": "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=\u27e83,1,k\u27e9$, $b=\u27e80,-2,-2\u27e9$, and $c=\u27e8-2,0,2\u27e9$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "b91ab935-69f2-5bb1-8f2f-f3eb2db7cdc8",
          ":answer-field/key": "field-1",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/id": "fb620e6a-37c1-5d60-ba45-3d4dda1c2ff1",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "-2"
            }
          ],
          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "fb620e6a-37c1-5d60-ba45-3d4dda1c2ff1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & k \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|)+((-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}|)+((k)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((3)\\cdot ((-2)\\cdot (2)-(-2)\\cdot (0)))+((-1)\\cdot ((0)\\cdot (2)-(-2)\\cdot (-2)))+((k)\\cdot ((0)\\cdot (0)-(-2)\\cdot (-2)))\\\\ &=((3)\\cdot (-4))+((-1)\\cdot (-4))+((k)\\cdot (-4))\\\\ &=(-12)+(4)+(-4k)\\\\ &=-4k-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4k-8&=0\\\\ -4k&=8\\\\ k&=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-2.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy"
    },
